<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
    <meta charset="UTF-8">
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0">
    <meta name="description" content="murmur - Open-source reliability harness for coding agents. Measure pass@1, pass^k, and variance to ship with confidence.">
    <title>murmur · Reliability Harness for Coding Agents</title>
    <link rel="stylesheet" href="styles.css">
</head>
<body>
    <header>
        <div class="container nav-container">
            <a href="#" class="logo">
                <span class="logo-icon">&#9679;</span> murmur
            </a>
            <nav aria-label="Main navigation">
                <a href="#features">Features</a>
                <a href="#metrics">Metrics</a>
                <a href="#testimonial">Testimonials</a>
            </nav>
        </div>
    </header>

    <main>
        <section class="hero">
            <div class="container">
                <h1>Ship Smarter with <span class="highlight">murmur</span></h1>
                <p class="hero-subtitle">The open-source reliability harness that brings production-grade confidence to your coding agents. Measure what matters before you deploy.</p>
                <div class="hero-cta">
                    <a href="#features" class="btn btn-primary">See How It Works</a>
                    <a href="#metrics" class="btn btn-outline">View Benchmarks</a>
                </div>
            </div>
        </section>

        <section id="metrics" class="metrics-strip" aria-labelledby="metrics-title">
            <div class="container">
                <h2 id="metrics-title" class="section-label">Real‑Time Reliability Snapshot</h2>
                <div id="metrics" class="metrics-grid">
                    <div class="metric-card">
                        <span class="metric-label">pass@1</span>
                        <span class="metric-value">94.7<span class="metric-unit">%</span></span>
                        <span class="metric-desc">First‑attempt accuracy across 12,400 coding tasks</span>
                    </div>
                    <div class="metric-card">
                        <span class="metric-label">pass<sup>k</sup></span>
                        <span class="metric-value">99.2<span class="metric-unit">%</span></span>
                        <span class="metric-desc">k‑sample consensus (k=10) with majority voting</span>
                    </div>
                    <div class="metric-card">
                        <span class="metric-label">variance</span>
                        <span class="metric-value">&plusmn;1.3<span class="metric-unit">%</span></span>
                        <span class="metric-desc">Run‑to‑run deviation across 50 repeated trials</span>
                    </div>
                </div>
            </div>
        </section>

        <section id="features" class="features-section" aria-labelledby="features-title">
            <div class="container">
                <h2 id="features-title" class="section-title">Why Teams Choose murmur</h2>
                <div class="features-grid">
                    <article class="feature-card">
                        <div class="feature-icon">&#9776;</div>
                        <h3>Consistent Pass@1 Tracking</h3>
                        <p>Every coding agent behaves differently under varying prompts. murmur standardizes evaluation so you always know whether a one‑shot response will compile, pass tests, and meet spec — before your users find out the hard way.</p>
                    </article>
                    <article class="feature-card">
                        <div class="feature-icon">&#9878;</div>
                        <h3>Pass<sup>k</sup> Consensus Sampling</h3>
                        <p>Single draws can be lucky. murmur runs your agent k times, applies majority voting, and reports consensus accuracy. You get a true picture of reliability under stochastic decoding, not just a cherry‑picked run.</p>
                    </article>
                    <article class="feature-card">
                        <div class="feature-icon">&#9881;</div>
                        <h3>Variance Dashboards</h3>
                        <p>Low pass@1 means nothing if variance is high. murmur tracks run‑to‑run deviation and flags instability so you can lock down temperature, seed, and model version with data‑backed decisions.</p>
                    </article>
                </div>
            </div>
        </section>

        <section id="testimonial" class="testimonial-section" aria-labelledby="testimonial-title">
            <div class="container">
                <h2 id="testimonial-title" class="section-title">Trusted by Engineering Teams</h2>
                <figure class="testimonial-block">
                    <blockquote>
                        <p>"We used murmur to validate our agent before a major enterprise rollout. It caught a 12% pass@1 drop after a model update that our unit tests completely missed. That single catch saved us from a embarrassing demo to 200 engineers."</p>
                    </blockquote>
                    <figcaption>
                        <span class="testimonial-name">Sasha Voss</span>
                        <span class="testimonial-role">Staff Engineer, Veridian AI</span>
                    </figcaption>
                </figure>
            </div>
        </section>
    </main>

    <footer>
        <div class="container footer-container">
            <div class="footer-brand">
                <span class="logo-icon">&#9679;</span> murmur
                <span class="footer-tagline">Open‑source agent reliability</span>
            </div>
            <div class="footer-links">
                <a href="#" aria-label="GitHub repository">GitHub</a>
                <a href="#" aria-label="Twitter / X">X</a>
                <a href="#" aria-label="Discord community">Discord</a>
                <a href="#" aria-label="Documentation">Docs</a>
            </div>
            <p class="footer-credit">MIT Licensed · Built by the agent‑engineering community</p>
        </div>
    </footer>
</body>
</html>

